-*- coding:utf-8 -*-

autocad-runtime-vlax-sleep-and-rest — the CAD-side protocol runtime crashed under AutoCAD accoreconsole
=======================================================================================================

Reported from: the Windows vendor-probe CI job
(gitlab job 15534582489, vendor:probes:autocad) against AutoCAD 2022
accoreconsole batch. All three probes reported "no verdict (backend
unreachable or crashed)". The --keep-workdir / --write-workdir-path
artifacts captured the exact protocol channel files.

Diagnosis (from the kept workdir)
---------------------------------

logs/debug.log:
    [CAD] runtime LOAD succeeded
    [CAD] host lacks &rest; keeping bootstrap 1-arg shadows + walk-rewriting normalize
    [CAD] entering autolisp-protocol-server-loop
    [CAD] server-loop CAUGHT error: nombre d'arguments insuffisants

protocol/stderr.txt (twice):
    ERROR protocol read: fonction incorrecte: VLAX-SLEEP

protocol/status.txt: STOPPED ; status.txt: 99

Two AutoCAD-specific runtime-portability bugs, both masked on BricsCAD:

1. vlax-sleep is undefined on AutoCAD.
   `vlax-sleep` is a BricsCAD extension; AutoCAD's AutoLISP has no such
   function (and `(vl-load-com)` does not add it). The runtime's
   sleep/yield primitive wrapped the call in `vl-catch-all-apply` — but
   vl-catch-all-apply CANNOT trap the error raised when a symbol has NO
   function definition at all: that error fires while resolving the
   symbol to an applicable subr, before the guard is established, so it
   escapes. The default yield mode is "VLAX-SLEEP", so the very first
   poll-yield in the server loop's read path threw, was caught by the
   loop's outer "ERROR protocol read:" handler, set status FAILED READ,
   and stopped the loop before it ever served a request.

2. `(princ "")` is a 1-arg call against a 2-arg shadow.
   The bootstrap shadows print/princ/prin1 with two required params
   `(obj file)`. On BricsCAD the template rewrites them to `(&rest args)`
   variadic; on AutoCAD (no &rest) the fixed 2-arg shadows are kept and a
   code-walker rewrites *user* `(princ x)` -> `(princ x nil)`. But the
   runtime's OWN loop tails `(princ "")` (autolisp-remote-io.lsp, the
   server loop and the selftest loop) are not user code and not walked,
   so they call the 2-arg shadow with one arg -> "nombre d'arguments
   insuffisants". This fired AFTER the loop had already set STOPPED
   (hence the mystery numeric status 99), inside the run-common outer
   catch.

Fix (alfe 1.6.4 / clautolisp 1.6.15)
------------------------------------

autolisp-front-end/source/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp
  * new `autolisp-host-has-fn` (probes the symbol table via atoms-family
    — the reliable way to test function existence, since
    vl-catch-all-apply can't trap an unbound-symbol error)
  * new `autolisp-delay-ms` (DELAY-command yield, no vlax dependency)
  * `autolisp-sleep-ms` now probes VLAX-SLEEP first, degrading to
    autolisp-delay-ms on hosts that lack it (AutoCAD)

autolisp-front-end/source/runtime/autolisp-remote-io.lsp
  * `autolisp-protocol-sleep-ms` gets the same probe-first guard
  * both loop tails `(princ "")` -> `nil` (the return value is discarded
    by the run-common outer catch)

No backend conditioning is needed: the fixes degrade gracefully on any
host by capability, so BricsCAD/clautolisp behaviour is unchanged. The
SBCL suite (303 checks) is unaffected — these .lsp files execute only
inside the CAD host.

Round 2 (alfe 1.6.6) — the DELAY fallback had the SAME escape bug
----------------------------------------------------------------

The 1.6.4 fix cleared both original crashes (job 15534641534:
"server-loop returned normally", no more arity error), but the probes
still got no verdict. New stderr:

    ERROR protocol read: fonction d'ordre incorrecte: COMMAND

The vlax-sleep fallback was `(vl-catch-all-apply 'command (list "_DELAY" ms))`.
On AutoCAD accoreconsole *applying* the `command` subr raises "fonction
d'ordre incorrecte: COMMAND" — and, exactly like an undefined vlax-sleep,
that error fires while resolving `command` for the apply, BEFORE the
vl-catch-all-apply guard is established, so it escapes and aborts the read
loop on the first yield.

Fix: yield with neither vlax-sleep nor (command). New
`autolisp-busy-wait-ms` spins on (getvar "DATE") (Julian day + fraction;
1 day = 86400000 ms) — a wall clock every host provides, read with no
command context, that never signals. `autolisp-delay-ms` now calls it;
`autolisp-sleep-ms` / `autolisp-protocol-sleep-ms` still prefer vlax-sleep
(BricsCAD) and fall back to it. Costs CPU (a spin), acceptable for the
probe runs; hosts with a real sleep never reach it.

(The opt-in "DELAY"/"SLEEP" yield modes in autolisp-protocol-yield still
carry the same latent apply-escape pattern, but they are not the default
and not exercised here — left for a later cleanup.)

Status
------

OPEN pending the next vendor:probes:autocad CI run. With the yield now
non-signalling, the loop should hold at READY, accept the probe forms,
and emit real verdicts. Any FAIL from there is a genuine
clautolisp-vs-AutoCAD divergence to reconcile.
